   "source": [
    "# Marketing Econometric Project: A Deep Dive into Advertising's Impact on Sales\n",
    "\n",
    "## Introduction\n",
    "\n",
    "**What is this project about?**\n",
    "\n",
    "This project aims to understand the relationship between advertising spend on different channels (TV, Radio, and Newspaper) and sales. We will use econometric techniques to build a predictive model that can not only forecast sales but also help in optimizing the advertising budget.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why is it important?**\n",
    "\n",
    "In today's competitive market, it's crucial for businesses to understand the effectiveness of their marketing campaigns. By analyzing the data, we can identify which advertising channels are providing the best return on investment and make data-driven decisions to maximize sales.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How will we do it?**\n",
    "\n",
    "We will follow a step-by-step approach:\n",
    "\n",
    "1.  **Data Exploration:** We'll start by exploring the dataset to understand its structure and the relationships between variables.\n",
    "2.  **Hypothesis Testing:** We'll formulate and test hypotheses to validate our assumptions.\n",
    "3.  **Correlation Analysis:** We'll examine the correlation between different advertising channels and sales.\n",
    "4.  **Regression Modeling:** We'll build a multiple linear regression model to predict sales based on advertising spend.\n",
    "5.  **Model Evaluation:** We'll evaluate the model's performance and interpret the results.\n",
    "6.  **Budget Optimization:** We'll use the model to find the optimal allocation of a £1000 budget to maximize sales."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "import statsmodels.api as sm\n",
    "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n",
    "from statsmodels.stats.outliers_influence import variance_inflation_factor\n",
    "from scipy.optimize import minimize\n",
    "\n",
    "# Set a consistent style for plots\n",
    "sns.set_style('whitegrid')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Data Exploration and Preprocessing\n",
    "\n",
    "**What are we doing?**\n",
    "\n",
    "We are loading the `advertising.csv` dataset and examining its structure, checking for missing values, and getting a general feel for the data.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why are we doing it?**\n",
    "\n",
    "This is a crucial first step in any data analysis project. It helps us understand the data we're working with and identify any potential issues that need to be addressed before we start modeling.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How are we doing it?**\n",
    "\n",
    "We'll use the pandas library to load the data and then use functions like `head()`, `info()`, and `describe()` to get an overview of the dataset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Load the dataset\n",
    "data = pd.read_csv('advertising.csv')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Display the first few rows of the dataset\n",
    "print(\"First 5 rows of the dataset:\")\n",
    "print(data.head())\n",
    "\n",
    "# Get information about the dataset\n",
    "print(\"\nDataset Information:\")\n",
    "print(data.info())\n",
    "\n",
    "# Get summary statistics of the dataset\n",
    "print(\"\nSummary Statistics:\")\n",
    "print(data.describe())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)\n",
    "\n",
    "**What are we doing?**\n",
    "\n",
    "We are visualizing the relationships between the variables in our dataset.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why are we doing it?**\n",
    "\n",
    "EDA helps us to uncover patterns, anomalies, and relationships in the data. This will help us to build a better model.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How are we doing it?**\n",
    "\n",
    "We'll use `seaborn` and `matplotlib` to create visualizations like pairplots and heatmaps."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Visualize the relationships between variables\n",
    "sns.pairplot(data, x_vars=['TV', 'Radio', 'Newspaper'], y_vars='Sales', height=5, aspect=0.7, kind='reg')\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Correlation Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "**What are we doing?**\n",
    "\n",
    "We are calculating the correlation between the variables in our dataset and visualizing it as a heatmap.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why are we doing it?**\n",
    "\n",
    "Correlation analysis helps us to understand the strength and direction of the linear relationship between two variables. A high correlation between an advertising channel and sales suggests that it might be a good predictor.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How are we doing it?**\n",
    "\n",
    "We'll use the `.corr()` method to calculate the correlation matrix and `seaborn.heatmap()` to visualize it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Calculate the correlation matrix\n",
    "corr_matrix = data.corr()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Plot the heatmap\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(8, 6))\n",
    "sns.heatmap(corr_matrix, annot=True, cmap='coolwarm', fmt=\".2f\")\n",
    "plt.title('Correlation Matrix')\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Hypothesis Testing\n",
    "\n",
    "**What are we doing?**\n",
    "\n",
    "We are formulating and testing a hypothesis about the relationship between TV advertising and sales.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why are we doing it?**\n",
    "\n",
    "Hypothesis testing provides a formal way to test our assumptions. In this case, we want to see if there is a statistically significant relationship between TV advertising and sales.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How are we doing it?**\n",
    "\n",
    "We'll perform a simple linear regression and look at the p-value for the TV coefficient.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Hypothesis:**\n",
    "\n",
    "*   **Null Hypothesis (H0):** There is no relationship between TV advertising and sales (the coefficient of TV is zero).\n",
    "*   **Alternative Hypothesis (H1):** There is a relationship between TV advertising and sales (the coefficient of TV is not zero)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Prepare the data for the simple linear regression model\n",
    "X_simple = data[['TV']]\n",
    "y = data['Sales']\n",
    "\n",
    "# Add a constant to the predictor\n",
    "X_simple_const = sm.add_constant(X_simple)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Fit the model\n",
    "model_simple = sm.OLS(y, X_simple_const).fit()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Print the model summary\n",
    "print(model_simple.summary())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Interpretation of Hypothesis Test:**\n",
    "\n",
    "The p-value for the TV coefficient is very close to zero (typically shown as `P>|t|` in the summary). This is much less than the standard significance level of 0.05. Therefore, we **reject the null hypothesis** and conclude that there is a statistically significant relationship between TV advertising and sales."
   ]
  }
 ]
